// The msdftext package (a working name; the owner names packages): text for
// the world, sharp at every size and angle, from TrueType fonts, as one
// multi-channel signed distance field (MSDF) atlas. A program reaches it with
// 'import msdftext', and gets:
//
// - Font (font.cone): a TrueType file read in place: glyf outlines (simple
//   and composite, quadratic), cmap (formats 0, 4, 6, 12), hmtx, hhea, head,
//   maxp, GPOS pair kerning (the 'kern' feature: lookup types 2 and 9) and
//   the legacy kern table, names. CFF outlines are NOT read yet (a cubic
//   solver and CFF's charstrings: 'Font.load' refuses an 'OTTO' file by name).
// - Shape, Edge, resolveOverlaps, colorEdges (shape.cone): an outline as
//   lines and quadratic Beziers in contours; overlapping and self-crossing
//   contours (a variable font's) resolved to the boundary of the filled area;
//   and msdfgen's edge colouring.
// - generateMsdf, generateSdf, correctErrors (msdf.cone): the field of a
//   coloured shape, on the CPU, after Viktor Chlumsky's msdfgen, and the
//   repair of its interpolation artifacts.
// - Atlas, SkylinePacker (atlas.cone): glyph fields generated on first use
//   into one RGBA8 image.
// - layoutText, TextLayout (layout.cone): code points to glyph quads: pair
//   kerning, line breaking at a width, left, centre and right alignment, line
//   height. No shaping (see layout.cone for what that would take).
// - TextRenderer, TextStyle, overlayTransform, worldTransform,
//   billboardPlacement (render.cone, msdftext.slang): the atlas on the GPU and
//   text drawn from it, as screen overlay at pixel sizes or placed in the
//   world (fixed or billboarded), with an outline and a drop shadow.
// - DEMO_FONT (demofont.cone): six geometric glyphs made for the tests,
//   public domain, so a program has a font to draw without a file.
//
// A frame, text on the screen and a label in the world (render's Post and
// Renderer, the latter keeping its depth with 'renderer.keepDepth = true'):
//
//   // once
//   imm font = Font.load(&DEMO_FONT)...;
//   mut atlas = Atlas.create(AtlasConfig.standard());
//   mut text = TextRenderer.create(&mut device, &atlas, TEXTURE_FORMAT_RGBA8_UNORM, TEXTURE_FORMAT_RGBA16_FLOAT,
//     TEXTURE_FORMAT_DEPTH32_FLOAT);
//   imm hud = text.addText(&mut device, SPACE_OVERLAY);
//   imm label = text.addText(&mut device, SPACE_WORLD);
//   // when the words change
//   imm layout = layoutText(&font, &mut atlas, "Score 10", &LayoutOptions.make(24.));
//   text.syncAtlas(&mut device, &atlas);
//   text.set(&mut device, hud, &layout, &TextStyle.plain(white), overlayTransform(w, h, 16., 16.));
//   // each frame
//   renderer.render(...);
//   text.renderWorld(&mut encoder, &post.hdr, &depth);     // before post
//   post.apply(&mut encoder, &target);
//   text.renderOverlay(&mut encoder, &target);              // after tone mapping
//
// Conventions: the layout's block space is x right, y DOWN, origin at the
// block's top left, in the units of the size given (pixels or metres).
// Everything is f32 but the field generation, which is f64.
//
// Its files are one module, and only this one, the root, imports:
//
//   font.cone      Font, Outline, FontError
//   shape.cone     Pt, Edge, Contour, Shape, colorEdges
//   msdf.cone      Field, Frame, generateMsdf, generateSdf, correctErrors
//   atlas.cone     AtlasConfig, GlyphEntry, SkylinePacker, Atlas
//   layout.cone    LayoutOptions, TextLayout, layoutText, decodeUtf8
//   render.cone    TextRenderer and the placement helpers; msdftext.slang's SPIR-V
//   demofont.cone  DEMO_FONT, DEMO_FONT_KERN (tools/makedemofont.py writes it)
//
// Fonts: no font is shipped but the demo one. Windows' Cascadia (OFL 1.1 in
// its source repository; the installed copy carries Microsoft's wording) and
// Arial (proprietary) are used by tests that skip when they are absent. The
// licence of any font a world embeds is open, and is not this package's to
// settle.

mod msdftext;

import libc;
import collections use *;
import geomath use *;
import gpu use *;
import render use Camera;
